  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)<0.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the factor $2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\u21d2\\,-4\\pi <2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi <-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince $2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)}{2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi } & >\\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x+3)-3\\pi } \\\\ 2x+9 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-9 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
